"Linearization Explains Fine-Tuning in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08239v1)\nciteturn31658view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view2\",\"lineno\":291}); Total lines: 810\nL281: To convert it into a binary classification task, we consider reviews with ratings less than 3 as label 0 (negative sentiment) and those with ratings greater than or equal to 3 as label 1 (positive sentiment).\nL282: Table cite76†9 in Appendix cite30†N shows specific hyperparameters for RoBERTa base across various benchmarks, including GLUE tasks (CoLA, SST-2), Yelp, and IMDb. For all experiments, we use LoRA on the RoBERTa-base model from the Hugging Face transformers library [cite77†26 ], and report its performance on different tasks. We implemented our code on NVIDIA Tesla V100 GPUs. Following Hu et al.\nL283: ($2022$), we mostly use the weights of the query and value layers, $\\mathbf{W}_{q}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times p}$ and $\\mathbf{W}_{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times p}$ for fine-tuning. In our experiments, we apply LoRA with $r=8$, which has $(m+p)\\times r=2\\times 768\\times 8$ trainable parameters per selected layer for each of the query, key, and value projection matrices in the self-attention mechanism in the RoBERTa base model.\nL284: cite78†Image: Refer to caption (a) Train loss over 10 epochs\nL285: \nL286: cite79†Image: Refer to caption (b) Condition number\nL287: \nL288: cite80†Image: Refer to caption (c) Evaluation accuracy and Condition number\nL289: Figure 2: (a)-(b) Illustrate the positive correlation between the convergence rate of optimization steps of LoRA over $10$ epochs and $\\kappa(\\mathbf{K}+\\sigma\\mathbf{I})$ of NTK at initialization. $\\{\\mathbf{W}_{q},\\mathbf{W}_{v}\\}$ of layers $\\{0,5,11\\}$ are fine-tuned. (c) Illustrates the negative correlation between evaluation accuracy after 10 epochs of training and the condition number of NTK. LoRA with $r=8$ is used to fine-tune $\\{\\mathbf{W}_{k}\\}$ of the layers $\\{0,5,11\\}$.\nL290: ### 6.2 Linearization of Fine-Tuning\nL291: In Table cite61†1 , we analyze how regularization impacts the fine-tuning of models on the CoLA and SST-2 datasets, using several metrics, including the deviation of fine-tuned parameters from the pretrained parameters, $\\|\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\|$, KL divergence between predictions of the regularized fine-tuned model and its linearized counterpart, the $\\ell_{2}$ norm $\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\bar{f}_{\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}}(\\mathbf{X})\\|_{2}$, evaluation accuracy.\nL292: The results indicate that increasing the regularization strength, $\\lambda$, reduces both the deviation in parameters and prediction divergence, measured by KL divergence and $\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\bar{f}_{\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}}(\\mathbf{X})\\|$, implying that stronger regularization keeps the fine-tuning behavior closer to its linearized approximation.\nL293: Conversely, as regularization weakens, $\\lambda\\rightarrow 0$, parameter deviations and divergences from linearization increase, suggesting a more significant departure from the linear regime. This is well aligned with the bounds in Theorems cite53†2 and cite60†4 . Notably, the overall accuracy remains largely unaffected by the regularization, and its fluctuations are within the error margin for $\\lambda$ up to 10.\nL294: The key takeaways from Table 1 are that, as long as we don’t overregularize ($\\lambda$ not too large, e.g., smaller than 50), we simultaneously: (1) encourage the model not to deviate (the direct objective of regularization), (2) encourage the NTK solution and the regularized fine-tuning solutions to become closer (the indirect main objective of regularization), and (3) do not sacrifice much on performance.\nL295: We conjecture that the reason we don’t sacrifice much on performance is that the fine-tuning solution is already relatively close to the initial model and that there are equivalently good NTK solutions in the same neighborhood.\nL296: (The choice of $\\lambda$ is similar to searching for solutions within sequentially smaller neighborhoods around the initialization, as $\\lambda$ increases.) The trifecta of easy implementation, no performance sacrifice, and theoretical compatibility with the NTK framework makes our proposed regularization an especially attractive choice.\nL297: ### 6.3 NTK Evaluation\nL298: We verify our proposition that by calculating the condition number of the NTK matrix for the LoRA model at initialization, we can predict the generalization error, including evaluation loss and accuracy. Table cite81†2 presents training loss, evaluation loss, accuracy, condition number of the NTK before fine-tuning, based on the snapshot at epoch 10.\nL299: We vary LoRA parameters across different layers ({0}, {11}, {0,11}, {0,5,11}) for query and value parameters, and layers {0,5,11} for key parameters, across various tasks and datasets. We collected $\\mathbf{X}=\\left[\\mathbf{x}_{1},\\mathbf{x}_{2},\\ldots,\\mathbf{x}_{n}\\right]^{\\top}$ using $n=32$ samples, randomly selected from the training datasets and computed $\\mathbf{k}(\\mathbf{x}_{i},\\mathbf{x}_{j})$ with respect to trainable parameters, $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ of LoRA.\nL300: The final empirical NTK matrix is $\\mathbf{K}(\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{X})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{32\\times 32}$. Our work is not specifically studying optimal sketching of the kernel matrix; however, in Appendix cite25†I , we empirically illustrate that our numerical results are robust to the choice of NTK samples. Note that the number of samples used for calculation of the empirical NTK is orders of magnitude smaller than the training dataset for sampling.\nL301: This shows that the results remain valid even with a sketch of the full kernel. This finding highlights the great potential of kernel methods for large language models (LLMs), particularly in terms of efficiency.\nL302: At first glance, in Table cite81†2 , the highest condition number does not correspond to the lowest accuracy. However, one may notice that this is an issue primarily when the nature (query/value vs. key) and number of parameters change . When this is the case, the effective complexity of the fine-tuned model changes, and since the NTK parameter $\\sigma$ is inversely proportional to the complexity, it affects the regularized condition number.\nL303: In the table, we have fixed $\\sigma$ and so this change is not being reflected and it is only fair to compare instead cases where the nature and number of parameters are the same, e.g., fixing a layer and varying tasks among CoLA, SST2, IMDB, YeLP. The correlation is perfect for $\\{0,11\\}$, but also mostly tracks for other fixed choices. This also holds when varying the same number of layers like $\\{0,7\\}$, $\\{0,11\\}$ for CoLA and SST2 in Fig. cite82†3 .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Statistical Framework for Alignment with Biased AI Feedback (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08259v1)\nciteturn31658view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view3\",\"lineno\":295}); Total lines: 458\nL282: Let $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{IPO}}(\\pi)$ denote the IPO estimator based only on $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{Human}}$. Then\nL283:  | $$\\mathrm{MSE}(\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi))<\\mathrm{MSE}(\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{IPO}}(\\pi)),\\qquad n,N\\to\\infty.$$  |\nL284: \nL285: Moreover, when $N\\gg n$, the estimator $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi)$ attains the semiparametric efficiency bound. In particular, it achieves the minimum asymptotic variance and mean squared error among all regular estimators of $P(\\pi(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot))$.\nL286: \nL287: Finally, we establish a regret bound for the resulting policy estimator.\nL288: ###### Theorem 3 (Regret bound for DIPO).\nL289: \nL290: Under the conditions of Theorem cite70†2 and Assumption cite57†3 , suppose $\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|\\to 0$ and $\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}-1\\|\\to 0$. Then\nL291:  |  | $\\displaystyle P(\\pi^{*}(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot))-P(\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot))$  |\nL292:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\quad=O\\!\\left(\\beta+\\sqrt{v\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{1}{N}\\right)}+v\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{1}{N}\\right)+\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}-1\\|\\cdot\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|\\right).$  |\nL293: Theorem cite71†3 derives the regret bound of DIPO and highlights several key factors influencing the performance of the proposed DIPO estimator. First, the bound decreases as the sample size increases, reflecting the expected improvement in performance as more preference data become available. Second, it grows with the regularization parameter $\\beta$ in the KL penalty, indicating that $\\beta$ should be chosen sufficiently small to limit its impact.\nL294: Most importantly, the dependence on estimation errors in the reference policy and the preference model appears only through the product $\\|\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}-1\\|\\,\\|\\tilde{g}-g\\|$ which is different from the regret of DDPO. This structure substantially reduces the impact of misspecification in either component and plays a central role in the robustness of the proposed method.\nL295: ## 5 Numerical Experiments\nL296: In this section, we present the numerical results for the proposed DDPO and DIPO methods. Our primary objective is to assess their effectiveness in learning policies using an extensive collection of biased AI-generated labels, along with a comparatively small set of high-quality human annotations.\nL297: We conduct experiments on three open-ended text generation tasks that are commonly used as benchmarks in LLM evaluation (cite28†Rafailov et al., 2023 ): controlled sentiment generation, summarization, and single-turn dialogue.\nL298: Specifically, across all tasks, $X$ denotes the input text provided to the LLM and $Y$ represents the corresponding text output generated by the LLM. In controlled sentiment generation, the input $X$ corresponds to a prefix of a movie review, and the LLM generates an output $Y$ that expresses positive sentiment. In summarization, $X$ consists of a forum post, and the LLM produces a summary $Y$ that captures the main points of the post.\nL299: In single-turn dialogue, $X$ denotes a user query, and the LLM generates a helpful response $Y$.\nL300: Across all tasks, we construct biased labeled datasets starting from a ground-truth dataset to assess the performance loss due to incomplete human supervision. Specifically, we begin with a ground-truth dataset $\\{(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},Z_{i})\\}_{i=1}^{N}$, where $Z_{i}$ denotes the human preference label.\nL301: We then simulate a noisy AI-labeled dataset, denoted by $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{AI}}$, by randomly flipping the preference labels for 40% of the pairs in the ground-truth dataset, thereby producing biased preference labels $\\widehat{Z}$.\nL302: Then, we randomly select 20% of the samples from $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{AI}}$ and re-annotate them with the corresponding ground-truth human $Z$, denoted by $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{Hum}}$, so that these samples contain both the noisy AI label $\\widehat{Z}$ and the true label $Z$, while the remaining 80% retain only the biased AI label $\\widehat{Z}$.\nL303: The simulated dataset represents a realistic scenario where a small number of high-quality human annotations are available alongside a large number of noisy AI-generated labels.\nL304: We emphasize that the source of the “human” preference signal varies across tasks and methods. For the controlled sentiment generation task, paired completions are generated on demand; therefore, we use a strong LLM evaluator as a proxy for human judgment for both DDPO and DIPO experiments. For the summarization and single-turn dialogue tasks, the source of human preference depends on the training procedure.\nL305: For DDPO, which operates on fixed datasets, we use the original human annotations provided in the benchmark datasets whenever available. In contrast, DIPO requires on-policy generation of new samples during training, for which ground-truth human annotations are unavailable; in this setting, we again employ strong LLM evaluators as proxies for human feedback. Detailed configurations of the evaluators and models are provided in the task-specific subsections.\nL306: Below, we provide descriptions of the three experimental tasks. Additional details on the dataset and prompts are put in Section D of the supplement material.\nL307: #### Controlled Sentiment Generation.\nL308: We use the IMDb dataset (cite72†Maas et al., 2011 ), which contains 25,000 training samples and 25,000 testing samples. We first fine-tune the gpt-neo-125m model (cite73†Gao et al., 2020 ) for three epochs on the training set to enable movie review generation. Using the resulting supervised fine-tuned (SFT) model as the reference policy, we generate paired completions for each prompt. For each completion pair, we use gpt-4o-mini to select the preferred output, which is treated as the human preference label.\nL309: The reinforcement learning algorithms are then trained for three epochs.\nL310: For evaluation, we randomly select a subset of 5,000 samples from the test set. Given an input prompt $X$, we generate one completion from the learned policy $\\pi$ and one completion $\\pi_{\\mathrm{SFT}}$ from the SFT reference policy. We utilize the pre-trained sentiment classifier sentiment-roberta-large-english (cite74†Hartmann et al., 2023 ) to evaluate the quality of the generations.\nL311: The evaluation score is defined as $s(X,Y)=\\mathbb{P}(\\text{positive}\\mid X,Y)$, representing the probability of positive sentiment. We calculate the win rate by directly comparing the sentiment scores of the two completions. Consequently, the win rate is defined as the proportion of test prompts for which the completion generated by $\\pi$ achieves a higher sentiment score than the completion generated by $\\pi_{\\mathrm{SFT}}$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNear-Oracle KV Selection via Pre-hoc Sparsity for Long-Context Inference (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08329v1)\nciteturn31658view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31650view2\",\"lineno\":192}); Total lines: 994\nL186: Definition 3.1 (Token-sparse attention, informal). At decoding step $t$, with KV budget $N<t$, a selector $\\mathcal{S}$ outputs an index set $S_{t}\\subseteq[t]\\coloneqq\\{1,\\dots,t\\}$ with $|S_{t}|=N$. The corresponding key/value matrices are restricted to these indices, $\\mathbf{K}_{S_{t}}$ and $\\mathbf{V}_{S_{t}}$. Attention is computed only between $\\mathbf{q}_{t}$ and $\\mathbf{K}_{S_{t}}$ (cf. Eq. (cite131†2 )), and the output uses $\\mathbf{V}_{S_{t}}$. The sparsity ratio at step $t$ is $N/t$.\nL187: Definition 3.2 (Key-value sharing). Given indices $S_{i}$ retrieved by $\\mathcal{S}$ at an anchor step $i$ in TSA, a later token at step $j$ may directly reuse them, denoted $S_{j}\\leftarrow S_{i}$. In this case, $S_{j}$ is utilized to construct $\\mathbf{K}_{S_{j}}$ and $\\mathbf{V}_{S_{j}}$.\nL188: Discussion. Different TSA instantiations correspond to different selectors $\\mathcal{S}$, and exhibit distinct accuracy–efficiency trade-offs under a fixed budget. For example, imprecise selection that admits many low-contribution pairs can crowd out salient entries, increasing approximation error and degrading model performance. This failure mode is analogous to erroneous KV sharing across steps whose truly critical sets have little overlap.\nL189: Moreover, selectors rely on different criteria [cite120†19 , cite113†49 , cite118†28 ] (e.g., similarity heuristics, learned scores, recency rules), and the mechanism used to enforce these criteria determines selection complexity and entry-retrieval efficiency. Consequently, an effective TSA selector must balance accuracy (retained mass) and retrieval overhead (selection complexity).\nL190: ### II-C Information Loss Quantification\nL191: To evaluate the accuracy of TSA selectors, we quantify the information loss—information gap between full attention and TSA—via a mutual-information (MI) bound, and pair it with retrieval-efficiency analysis to guide algorithm design. We first derive the MI bound as a function of dropped attention mass, which implies that the top-$k$ oracle attains the best accuracy under a fixed budget. However, it incurs prohibitive selection cost due to explicit attention evaluation.\nL192: Practical selectors approximate this oracle for efficiency and thus incur additional MI loss. We then formalize selector design as an optimization problem towards minimizing the MI-loss gap relative to the top-$k$ oracle subject to retrieval constraints. Finally, we show that PrHS-based selectors remove posterior bias and, with design-time error control, nearly preserve top-$k$ accuracy without additional retrieval cost, thereby outperforming PoHS selectors.\nL193: ##### Mutual-information Bound\nL194: In TSA, a selector $\\mathcal{S}$ keeps $N$ KV entries and drops the rest. Our core result (Sec. cite59†VII ) is that the MI loss induced by TSA depends only on the dropped mass, independent of selector instantiations. Intuitively, preserving (nearly) the same total attention mass as full attention preserves (nearly) the same information. Hence, MI loss admits a unified characterization in terms of dropped mass. For a query $\\mathbf{q}$, define the retained and dropped attention mass\nL195:  | $$\\tau_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\mathbf{q})=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{S}}A_{i}(\\mathbf{q}),\\quad\\delta_{\\mathcal{S}}(q)=1-\\tau_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\mathbf{q}).$$  |  | (3)\nL196: \nL197: Let $I$ denote the MI loss measure. For any $\\mathbf{q}$, the MI loss between full attention $I_{\\mathrm{full}}$ and selector $\\mathcal{S}$ satisfies\nL198:  | $\\displaystyle 0\\,\\leq$  | $\\displaystyle I_{\\mathrm{full}}(\\mathbf{q})-I_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\mathbf{q})\\,\\leq\\,g\\big(\\delta_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\mathbf{q})\\big),$  |  | (4)\nL199:  | $\\displaystyle\\text{where}$  | $\\displaystyle g(\\delta)\\coloneqq 2\\left[h_{\\mathrm{b}}(\\delta)+\\delta\\log L\\right].$  |\nL200: \nL201: Here $h_{\\mathrm{b}}$ is the binary entropy function and $L$ the number of eligible positions (proved in Sec. cite61†VII-B ).\nL202: ##### Top-k Oracle and Optimization Goal\nL203: \nL204: The top-$k$ oracle $\\mathcal{S}^{*}(q)\\!=\\!\\operatorname*{Top}_{N}\\!\\big(A(\\mathbf{q})\\big)$ minimizes the bound by maximizing retained mass under a fixed budget $N$:\nL205: \nL206:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{S}^{*}(\\mathbf{q})\\!\\in\\!\\operatorname*{argmin}_{S\\subseteq[t],\\,|S|=N}g\\!\\left(1\\!-\\!\\!\\sum_{i\\in S}A_{i}(\\mathbf{q})\\right)\\!=\\!\\operatorname*{argmax}_{S\\subseteq[t],\\,|S|=N}\\sum_{i\\in S}A_{i}(\\mathbf{q}).$  |  | (5)\nL207: However, evaluating $A(\\mathbf{q})$ exactly incurs prohibitive selection cost. Practical selectors (PoHS or PrHS) approximate $A(\\mathbf{q})$ for efficiency and thus incur additional MI loss. We therefore pose the goal of selector optimization as\nL208: \nL209:  | $$\\min\\;\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\,I_{\\mathcal{S}^{*}}(\\mathbf{q})\\;-\\;I_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\mathbf{q})\\,\\right]\\;\\;\\text{s.t.}\\;\\;|S^{*}(\\mathbf{q})|=|S(\\mathbf{q})|=N,$$  |  | (6)\nL210: i.e., minimize the MI-loss gap relative to the top-$k$ oracle under constrained budget, where the expectation is over queries $\\mathbf{q}$.\nL211: ##### PoHS Selector $\\mathcal{S}_{D}$\nL212: \nL213: $\\mathcal{S}_{D}(\\mathbf{q})\\!=\\!\\operatorname*{Top}_{n}(\\widehat{A}_{D}(\\mathbf{q}))$ replaces $A(\\mathbf{q})$ with its surrogate $\\widehat{A}_{D}(\\mathbf{q})$ derived from posterior side information $D$ (e.g., token ages, sketches), and suffers posterior bias measured by the total-variation distance\nL214: \nL215:  | $$\\varepsilon_{D}(\\mathbf{q})\\coloneqq\\tfrac{1}{2}\\|A(\\mathbf{q})-\\widehat{A}_{D}(\\mathbf{q})\\|_{1}.$$  |  | (7)\nL216: The extra dropped-mass gap satisfies $\\delta_{\\mathcal{S}_{D}}(\\mathbf{q})\\leq\\delta^{*}(\\mathbf{q})+2\\varepsilon_{D}(\\mathbf{q})$, which, by the monotonicity^{1}^{1} 1 Without loss of generality, in this paper, we restrict the domain of $g$ to the interval (0, L/(1+L)] to ensure its monotonicity, where L denotes the current text length. Note that as $L$ grows large, $L/(1+L)$ will approach $1$, which renders the previous restriction reasonable. of $g$ in Eq. (cite134†4 ), lifts the MI loss bound to\nL217:  | $$I_{\\mathrm{full}}(\\mathbf{q})-I_{\\text{post}}(\\mathbf{q})\\ \\leq\\ g\\big(\\delta^{*}(\\mathbf{q})+2\\varepsilon_{D}(\\mathbf{q})\\big).$$  |  | (8)\nL218: Thus, when the surrogate under- or over-scores tokens (large $\\varepsilon_{D}$), the selected set retains less probability mass than the oracle and loses more information. We formalize both TDO and QAA from Sec. cite6†I as PoHS instances. In general, TDO induces larger $\\varepsilon_{D}$ than QAA (worse accuracy) but avoids explicit evaluation of $A(\\mathbf{q})$ (lower retrieval cost). Proofs are provided in Sec. cite61†VII-B .\nL219: ##### PrHS Selector $\\mathcal{S}_{pre}$\nL220: \nL221: $\\mathcal{S}_{pre}$ selects critical set without explicitly computing $A(\\mathbf{q})$ and controls a theoretical error $\\beta_{\\mathrm{th}}(q)\\!\\geq\\!0$ that satisfy\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOJBKQ: Objective-Joint Babai-Klein Quantization (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08376v1)\nciteturn31658view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31650view3\",\"lineno\":115}); Total lines: 516\nL100:  | (Runtime-consistent)     | $\\displaystyle\\min_{\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}}\\;\\;\\|\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}-\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\mathbf{W}\\|_{F}^{2}$  |  | (1)\nL101:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\qquad=\\|\\widehat{\\mathbf{Y}}-\\mathbf{Y}^{\\mathrm{rt}}\\|_{F}^{2},$  |  | (2)\nL102:  | (Full-precision mapping)  | $\\displaystyle\\min_{\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}}\\;\\;\\|\\mathbf{X}\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}-\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{W}\\|_{F}^{2},$  |  | (3)\nL103:  | (Mismatch target)  | $\\displaystyle\\min_{\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}}\\;\\;\\|\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}-\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{W}\\|_{F}^{2}$  |  | (4)\nL104:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\qquad=\\|\\widehat{\\mathbf{Y}}-\\mathbf{Y}^{\\mathrm{fp}}\\|_{F}^{2}.$  |  | (5)\nL105: Eq. (cite61†1 ) directly reflects runtime behavior but aligns to a target computed on quantized activations, which may be noisy; this objective is adopted by GPTQ and QUIP. Eq. (cite62†3 ) admits a convenient least-squares structure (prior to discretization) but suffers from train–run mismatch when earlier layers are quantized, and is optimized by AWQ.\nL106: Eq. (cite63†4 ) partially alleviates this mismatch by evaluating candidates on runtime activations while preserving the full-precision reference; QEP employs this objective as a corrective patch within a layer-wise quantization pipeline, although it may over-penalize candidates when error drift accumulates across layers.\nL107: These trade-offs motivate a unified selection objective that interpolates between runtime-consistent and full-precision references, while optionally controlling weight drift.\nL108: \nL109: We propose an expressive Joint Target Alignment (JTA) objective with a single continuous knob to score and select candidates generated by Random-$K$ decoding. We define an interpolated target\nL110:  | $$\\small\\mathbf{Y}^{\\star}(\\mu):=(1-\\mu)\\,\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{W}+\\mu\\,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\mathbf{W},\\hskip 18.49988pt\\mu\\in[0,1],$$  |  | (6)\nL111: \nL112: and formulate the ILS problem:\nL113: \nL114:  | $$\\min\\mathcal{S}(\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}})\\;:=\\;\\|\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}-\\mathbf{Y}^{\\star}(\\mu)\\|_{F}^{2}\\;+\\;\\lambda^{2}\\|\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}-\\mathbf{W}\\|_{F}^{2},$$  |  | (7)\nL115: where $\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}$ is subject to some constraint. Later we will show this is a multiple-right-hand-side box-constrained integer least squares (BILS) problem and provide a sub-optimal solution. The knob $\\mu$ interpolates between matching full-precision and runtime-consistent references, while $\\lambda$ optionally regularizes weight displacement.\nL116: In our framework, Random-$K$ quantization (Sec. cite16†3.4 ) rewrites find a sub-optimal solution to , and JTA (Eq. (cite43†7 )) provides a consistent curvature-aware criterion for selecting the best candidate under error propagation. When $\\mu{=}1$ and $\\lambda{=}0$, Eq. (cite43†7 ) reduces to the runtime-consistent objective in Eq. (cite61†1 ); when $\\mu{=}0$ and $\\lambda{=}0$, it aligns to the full-precision reference in Eq. (cite63†4 ).\nL117: ##### End-to-end layer-wise procedure.\nL118: \nL119: For each layer, we (i) form $\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}$ by a partially quantized forward pass, (ii) find a sub-optimal optimal solution to Eq. (cite43†7 ), to be seen in the next sections. This closes the loop between discrete candidate generation and objective-aligned selection.\nL120: ### 3.2 From Layer-wise Quantization to Integer Least Squares\nL121: \nL122: From (cite43†7 ) we obtain\nL123: \nL124:  | $$\\mathcal{S}(\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}})\\;:=\\;\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\\\\nL125: \\lambda\\mathbf{I}\\end{bmatrix}\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\mathbf{Y}^{\\star}(\\mu)\\\\\nL126: \\lambda\\mathbf{W}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|_{F}^{2}.$$  |  | (8)\nL127: The task of finding an optimal quantized weight $\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}$ to minimize the selection objective in Sec. cite12†3.1 can be formally mapped to the classical Integer Least Squares (ILS) problem.\nL128: \nL129: Figure 1: Layer-wise comparison of the original output norms and JTA reconstruction errors across Layers 1, 15, and 30.We present results for all linear modules under varying $K$ settings.\nL130: \nL131: To facilitate the derivation, we first introduce additional notation. Let\nL132:  | $$\\mathbb{B}=\\{0,1,\\ldots,2^{\\text{wbit}}-1\\}$$  |\nL133: \nL134: denote the box constraint corresponding to the set of admissible integer values for a wbit-bit quantized weight. Let\nL135: \nL136:  | $$\\mathbf{Q}=[\\mathbf{q}_{1},\\mathbf{q}_{2},\\ldots,\\mathbf{q}_{n}]\\in\\mathbb{B}^{m\\times n}$$  |\nL137: \nL138: denote the quantized version of the full-precision weight matrix $\\mathbf{W}$. We further define\nL139:  | $$\\mathbf{S}=[\\mathbf{s}_{1},\\mathbf{s}_{2},\\ldots,\\mathbf{s}_{n}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n},\\quad\\mathbf{Z}=[\\mathbf{z}_{1},\\mathbf{z}_{2},\\ldots,\\mathbf{z}_{n}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}$$  |\nL140: \nL141: as the scale matrix and zero-point matrix, respectively. Both $\\mathbf{S}$ and $\\mathbf{Z}$ are pre-computed using standard statistical calibration methods (e.g., the Absmax method).\nL142: \nL143: Finally, the dequantized weight matrix is given by\nL144: \nL145:  | $$\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}=\\mathbf{S}\\odot(\\mathbf{Q}-\\mathbf{Z}),$$  |\nL146: where $\\odot$ denotes element-wise multiplication. The matrices $\\mathbf{Q}$, $\\mathbf{S}$, $\\mathbf{Z}$, and $\\widehat{\\mathbf{W}}$ are all partitioned column-wise in the same manner as $\\mathbf{W}$. We further define\nL147: \nL148:  | $$\\mathbf{A}:=\\begin{bmatrix}\\widetilde{\\mathbf{X}}\\\\\nL149: \\lambda\\mathbf{I}\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad\\mathbf{T}:=\\begin{bmatrix}\\mathbf{Y}^{\\star}(\\mu)\\\\\nL150: \\lambda\\mathbf{W}\\end{bmatrix}.$$  |\nL151: \nL152: Using the above notation, Eq. (cite64†8 ) can be rewritten as\nL153:  | $$\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\left\\|\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{D}_{j}\\mathbf{q}_{j}-\\mathbf{b}_{j}\\right\\|_{2}^{2},$$  |\nL154: \nL155: where $\\mathbf{D}_{j}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\mathbf{s}_{j})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times m}$ is a diagonal scaling matrix, and\nL156: \nL157:  | $$\\mathbf{b}_{j}=\\mathbf{t}_{j}+\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{D}_{j}\\mathbf{z}_{j}$$  |\nL158: \nL159: with $\\mathbf{t}_{j}$ denoting the $j$-th column of $\\mathbf{T}$.\nL160: \nL161: As a result, Eq. (cite64†8 ) decomposes into $n$ independent box-constrained integer least squares (BILS) problems:\nL162:  | $$\\min_{\\mathbf{q}_{j}\\in\\mathbb{B}^{m}}\\left\\|\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{D}_{j}\\mathbf{q}_{j}-\\mathbf{b}_{j}\\right\\|_{2}^{2},\\qquad j=1,\\ldots,n.$$  |  | (9)\nL163: \nL164: Since each subproblem is independent, we simplify notation by focusing on a single per-column subproblem:\nL165: \nL166:  | $$\\min_{\\mathbf{q}\\in\\mathbb{B}^{m}}\\left\\|\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{D}\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{b}\\right\\|_{2}^{2}.$$  |  | (10)\nL167: Solving Eq. (cite65†10 ) exactly is NP-hard for general lattices (cite55†Micciancio, 2002 ). A classical and efficient approximation is given by the Babai point (cite41†Babai, 1986 ), which performs successive orthogonal projections followed by greedy rounding along a triangular factorization of $\\mathbf{A}$. It matches the recent analyses (cite40†Chen et al., 2025 ) which suggest that layer-wise solvers such as GPTQ can be interpreted as greedy lattice decoding methods under alternative objective formulations.\n"
